<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04UP">Warning <span data-tag="04UP">10.2.1.27</span>.</a> The terminology of Definition <a data-tag="04UN" href="/tag/04UN">10.2.1.26</a> has the potential to create confusion. Since the forgetful functor $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ creates colimits (Proposition <a data-tag="02KB" href="/tag/02KB">7.1.4.26</a>), a sieve $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{0}_{/X} \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$ which is dense in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="04UN" href="/tag/04UN">10.2.1.26</a> is also dense when regarded as a full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="03VD" href="/tag/03VD">8.4.1.5</a>). Beware that the converse is false in general (Example <a data-tag="04UQ" href="/tag/04UQ">10.2.1.28</a>). However, it is true if $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ admits finite products (Proposition <a data-tag="04UR" href="/tag/04UR">10.2.1.29</a>). </p>
